WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:06.061
From vengeful fathers to parasitic aliens,
some famous movies just come before their time.

00:00:06.061 --> 00:00:09.862
These horror movies are the scream of the
crop of box office flops.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.510
The Sam Neill and Laurence Fishburne-led horror
Event Horizon is nothing short of an unforgettable

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.600
descent into hellish madness.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.619
The film follows the crew of a starship in
the year 2047, which is tasked with answering

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:27.350
the distress signal of another space-faring
vessel called the Event Horizon.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.900
This mysterious ship had suddenly disappeared
seven years prior during its maiden voyage

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.250
and has now eerily reappeared in orbit around
Neptune.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.710
What the crew finds on the Event Horizon is
the stuff of nightmares.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.000
The crew has been massacred, and the ship's
visual records depict the crew members losing

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.410
their minds and engaging in sexual activity
and body mutilation.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.818
The rescue crew then begins seeing horrific
hallucinations that recall their own individual histories.

00:00:52.818 --> 00:00:57.070
The mysteries of the Event Horizon all revolve
around its strange disappearance.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.510
Experimental space travel effectively took
the ship to another dimension: Hell.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.061
"Where we're going, we won't need eyes to
see."

00:01:06.061 --> 00:01:12.805
Anderson was riding high after the success
of the 1995 movie adaptation of the popular

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:18.540
video game "Mortal Kombat" but shifting from
PG-13 material to R-rated horror proved to

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.470
be financially detrimental for the director.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:27.810
Upon its 1997 release, Event Horizon only
grossed nearly $42 million worldwide against

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.420
a $60 million budget.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.822
Critically, it was panned for its excessive
gore according to the Rotten Tomatoes Critics Consensus.

00:01:34.822 --> 00:01:39.960
However, horror fans began flocking to the
film in droves as years passed, causing multiple

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:45.619
home editions to be released and spurring
on another examination of the film's critical standing.

00:01:45.619 --> 00:01:51.630
Even casual film fans around at the time can
recall the stir generated by The Sixth Sense.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:56.290
The 1999 film about a young boy who can see
dead people put director M. Night Shyamalan

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:57.290
on the map.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.970
However, there was another film released that
year featuring a protagonist who could see

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:06.060
the dead, and it was fatefully overshadowed
by the swelling popularity of "The Sixth Sense."

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.280
In "Stir of Echoes," Tom Witzky, played by
Kevin Bacon, is a blue collar phone line repairman

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.810
working in the suburbs of Chicago.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.920
He's a skeptic of the supernatural but agrees
to allow his sister-in-law to hypnotize him.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.780
During the alleged hypnosis, she suggests
that Tom be more "open-minded."

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.200
From this point forward, he begins seeing
visions of a ghostly apparition of a 17-year-old

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.610
girl, who wants Tom to help her complete some
unfinished business.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.280
While the film wasn't a complete financial
failure, it wasn't a success either and only

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.720
garnered a $21 million gross, according to
Box Office Mojo.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.610
The film actually received praise from critics
thanks to its great cast, tension-filled story,

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:50.140
and solid source material, as it's based on
horror master Richard Matheson's 1958 novel

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.510
A Stir of Echoes.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.200
However, many noted that it was just released
too closely to The Sixth Sense since it came

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.640
out just a month after Shyamalan's hit.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.030
The film simply suffered from hitting the
big screen at the wrong time.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:06.300
But once "The Sixth Sense" phenomenon died
down, horror fans were able to rediscover

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.890
"Stir of Echoes" after its home video release.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:13.590
Despite its middling Rotten Tomatoes scores,
Stir of Echoes continues to be a subject of

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.950
discussion for film critics and horror fans
alike.

00:03:17.314 --> 00:03:22.670
Based on Jeff VanderMeer's novel of the same
name, the Alex Garland-directed sci-fi psychological

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:28.060
horror "Annihilation" perplexed movie-goers
with its enigmatic story and underlying philosophy.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:33.000
The film posits that alien contact might not
be the cut-and-dry scenario we've always envisioned.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.220
A meteor crash lands on Earth and creates
an ever-expanding zone the government has

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.840
dubbed "the Shimmer."

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.420
Those who've gone inside the Shimmer haven't
returned, until one soldier named Kane, played

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.600
by Oscar Isaac finally does.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.340
However, he's been affected by the zone and
enters a coma.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:54.390
His wife Lena, played by Natalie Portman,
is a biologist and volunteers to join an expedition

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.519
into the Shimmer in hopes of finding answers
about her husband's condition.

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:00.000
"When did he arrive back home?"

00:04:03.143 --> 00:04:04.365
"I wanna see a lawyer."

00:04:04.365 --> 00:04:06.159
"You're not gonna be able to see a lawyer."

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:10.250
What the group finds is that life inside the
Shimmer has been mutated.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.520
Horrific animal monstrosities attack the group.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:17.320
They even find the bodies of those who have
gone before, albeit heavily disfigured.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.480
As Lena makes her way to ground zero, she
begins to learn a bit about herself and her

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.350
relationship with her husband.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:27.509
While Annihilation may have been a critical
darling for its provocative themes, the audiences

00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:29.250
simply didn't show up.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:35.100
With a production budget of $40 million and
a worldwide gross of $43 million, the studio

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.110
likely lost money once marketing costs were
factored in.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.470
Publications like Indiewire and The Guardian
argued that the movie suffered because of

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.590
the studio's fear of not seeing immediate
returns, which meant it didn't get time to

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:47.880
build an audience.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.120
However, thanks to the source material's popularity
among sci-fi readers and the film's release

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:57.260
on Netflix overseas, Annihilation found a
second chance at life with its streaming release.

00:04:57.802 --> 00:05:02.130
If you have a tough stomach, then maybe you
can handle the grisly imagery of the 2015

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.580
western horror Bone Tomahawk.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.810
It's the 1890s and Sheriff Franklin Hunt learns
that a tribe of cannibalistic cave-dwellers

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.940
who have been shunned by other indigenous
communities are the likely culprits of the

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.110
recent kidnappings of three people.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:19.490
The Sheriff, played by Kurt Russell, sets
out on a rescue mission with local gunslinger

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:25.430
John Brooder, played by Matthew Fox, and Deputy
Chicory, played by Richard Jenkins.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:29.570
Also along for the journey is the injured
Arthur O'Dwyer, played by Patrick Wilson,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.870
whose wife is one of the people that has been
captured.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.979
What they encounter, however, is the stuff
of nightmares.

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:39.550
While some horror fans can enjoy over-the-top
gore in a campy slasher, the violence in this

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.860
film is visceral, grimy, and jarring.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:44.150
Don't say you weren't warned.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:49.546
Bone Tomahawk received critical praise, and
even has a certified fresh rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:05:49.673 --> 00:05:54.556
Most enjoyed the slow-burn that paid off with
the intense tension and horrors of the third act.

00:05:54.556 --> 00:05:58.710
It's hard to say that this film was a total
flop, considering it only received a limited

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:04.740
theatrical release that made over $475,000
in ticket sales, according to The Numbers.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:08.930
For a release plan like this, it was always
intended to play the long game.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:13.289
While the ticket sales were mighty low, home
video sales spiked due to good word of mouth

00:06:13.289 --> 00:06:17.520
which, according to The Guardian, ensured
that the film was ultimately profitable in

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:18.520
the long run.

00:06:18.884 --> 00:06:22.780
When you hear the name Peter Jackson, you
likely think of his big budget epics like

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.850
The Lord of the Rings trilogy or King Kong.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:29.190
For this reason, many have been left in the
dark about Jackson's zombie horror comedy

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:32.979
film, Braindead, which was released in North
America with the title Dead Alive.

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:37.650
This film relishes in its namesake and brings
buckets full of gore and gross-out practical

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:39.710
effects to its ridiculous story.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.479
Lionel, played by Timothy Balme, is a young
man, who is taking care of his mother Vera,

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:46.220
played by Elizabeth Moody.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:50.880
Unfortunately for Lionel, Vera is extremely
overbearing and wants to dominate Lionel's

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.300
life at every turn.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:56.090
When Lionel lands a date with a young lady
he takes to the Wellington Zoo, his mother

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:57.370
follows them.

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.250
While there, she is bitten by a repulsive
creature.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:04.630
Vera soon succumbs to an illness stemming
from the bite that turns her into a zombie.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:08.620
She winds up infecting countless others and
poor Lionel is left to deal with the burgeoning

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.220
undead situation.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:17.928
The film only grossed $242,623 at the North
American box office, according to Box Office Mojo.

00:07:17.928 --> 00:07:22.410
However, the film became such a cult hit with
horror fans that it has continually been rereleased

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:28.750
on several platforms including VHS, Laserdisc,
DVD, and Blu Ray.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:32.629
According to The Hollywood Reporter, Jackson
wants to use his super technologically advanced

00:07:32.629 --> 00:07:37.800
Weta Digital VFX studio to remaster the film
for a 4K release.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.910
Dead Alive is an absolute blast, but just
make sure you're not eating anything while

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:42.910
viewing this romp.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:48.220
The slasher subgenre of horror wouldn't be
what it is today without John Carpenter's

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.229
iconic 1978 film, Halloween.

00:07:51.229 --> 00:07:55.319
The movie created a larger-than-life movie
monster in the form of the twisted serial

00:07:55.319 --> 00:07:56.669
killer, Michael Myers.

00:07:56.669 --> 00:08:01.509
The indie film's popularity prompted a sequel
that was released in 1981 and directed by

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:03.000
Rick Rosenthal.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.759
With Michael Myers established as a symbol
of horror, one would think that a three-quel

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:09.250
would center around his terrifying return.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.940
Instead, the filmmakers and producers opted
for an anthology approach to the Halloween

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:17.270
series, where each film would tell a different
story on Halloween.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:21.620
Carpenter felt that Michael Myers' story was
complete with Halloween II, so Halloween III

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:25.460
focused on a new cast of characters and a
Michael Myers-free tale.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:30.000
The problem is that the franchise already
had two films under its belt featuring Michael Myers.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.641
Audiences had inextricably linked the killer
with the Halloween name.

00:08:33.641 --> 00:08:38.020
Once movie goers realized he wasn't featured
in the third installment, the film's box office

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.289
performance waned significantly.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:45.470
Box Office Mojo reports that Halloween III
received a paltry $14 million gross compared

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:51.260
to the $25 million of its predecessor and
$47 million of the first film.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.519
Such a significant decrease in profit prompted
filmmakers to revert back to Michael Myers

00:08:55.519 --> 00:08:57.101
with the fourth installment.

00:08:57.101 --> 00:08:58.422
"Michael…"

00:09:01.732 --> 00:09:06.510
Halloween III ditched the slasher genre entirely
and embraced a different kind of horror that

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:11.290
straddles the lines between the supernatural
and sci-fi: Haunted masks imprinted with a

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:15.399
villainous company's microchip wreak havoc
on an unsuspecting population.

00:09:15.399 --> 00:09:19.470
In recent years, fans have returned to the
film praising its unique narrative.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:23.890
The film has seen new releases, including
a 4K release from Shout Factory.

00:09:25.191 --> 00:09:29.490
Director Sam Raimi is no stranger to horror,
with his Evil Dead franchise counted among

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.300
one of the most beloved of the genre.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.620
In this installment, we follow Ash, played
by Bruce Campbell, a demon-slaying, chainsaw-wielding

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:40.209
hero whose ego rivals only his own penchant
for survival.

00:09:40.209 --> 00:09:45.570
While 1981's Evil Dead started out as a small
indie horror film, it quickly gained traction

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:52.070
and led to the 1986 sequel Evil Dead II: Dead
by Dawn, which moved from pure horror to a

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:56.690
horror-comedy combination that fans have affectionately
dubbed as "splatstick."

00:09:56.690 --> 00:10:02.424
It was only a matter of time before the franchise
continued, which it did with 1993's Army of Darkness.

00:10:02.424 --> 00:10:06.470
This film pits Ash against the deadites of
old in a medieval civilization.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:10.339
Ash must live up to his name as the prophesied
champion and vanquish evil.

00:10:10.339 --> 00:10:14.089
While the film had the biggest budget of the
original trilogy, it didn't exactly bring

00:10:14.089 --> 00:10:16.670
home the bacon during its theatrical run.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:23.470
Despite its $11 million budget, Army of Darkness
only pulled in a measly $11.5 million in North

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.430
America and $21.5 million worldwide.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:31.040
Still, like the film that started it all,
Army of Darkness became a cult classic.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.840
In fact, Army of Darkness on home video became
a gateway for a whole new generation to discover

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:37.399
the Evil Dead films.

00:10:37.399 --> 00:10:43.380
Eventually, fan fervor pushed interested parties
into making a successful reboot film in 2013,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.839
as well as the Starz's TV series Ash vs Evil
Dead.

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:50.940
There's more "Evil Dead" on the way, as Evil
Dead Rise is scheduled to hit theaters in

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.910
the spring of 2023.

00:10:53.884 --> 00:10:59.290
There's nothing quite like a good old fashioned
story of revenge and murder, and 1988's Pumpkinhead

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.450
serves up vengeance nice and cold.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:05.950
Ed Harley, played by Lance Henriksen, is a
widower and single parent of his young son

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:07.730
Billy, played by Matthew Hurley.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:12.260
One day, while Ed is away for work, a group
of misfit teens begin to pick on Billy.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.410
They accidentally fatally injure the boy and
flee the scene.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:19.830
When Ed returns, he finds his deceased son
and soon learns who killed Billy.

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:24.340
The grieving Ed visits a witch and asks her
to help him seek vengeance upon the teens.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:31.290
"You called upon this thing, in that man's
name, and that man, he'd be avenged."

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:36.170
She summons a demonic creature known as Pumpkinhead,
who systematically hunts and kills the teens

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:37.520
one by one.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:41.320
This is another film that suffered because
it was released in a limited number of theaters,

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:46.149
which led to Pumpkinhead only grossing $4
million at the box office as a result.

00:11:46.149 --> 00:11:51.060
However, the film eventually found success
through home video releases, as new fans discovered

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:56.040
this terrifying and emotional film directed
by Stan Winston, the special effects mastermind

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.089
behind Aliens and The Terminator.

00:11:58.089 --> 00:12:02.880
Its home video success enabled the film to
receive a handful of sequels which, unfortunately,

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.320
don't remotely live up to the original film.

00:12:05.931 --> 00:12:11.350
George A. Romero, the father of the modern
zombie, is a titan among horror fandom. 1968's

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:15.810
Night of the Living Dead became memorable
for its graphic violence and depiction of

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.460
a world besieged by the cannibalistic undead.

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:20.720
In many ways, it was a first of its kind.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:26.100
The horror maestro succeeded that first outing
with 1978's Dawn of the Dead, a zombie horror

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:30.649
based in a mall, rife with biting commentary
on commercialism.

00:12:30.649 --> 00:12:35.160
Romero then plunged ahead with 1985's Day
of the Dead, which condemned the U.S. military's

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:39.190
might and penchant for shooting first and
asking questions later.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:43.230
Day of the Dead takes place in a bunker, where
a group of scientists and soldiers try to

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:47.889
survive and find a cure for the zombie plague,
but it turns out, the human threat is almost

00:12:47.889 --> 00:12:49.570
as serious as the zombie one.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:50.870
"We need each other.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:52.130
Can't we just get along—"

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:54.199
"You need us, the way I see it, lady.

00:12:54.199 --> 00:12:56.848
I'm not so sure we need you at all."

00:12:56.848 --> 00:13:02.370
Initially, the film only pulled in a worldwide
total of $5 million against a $3.5 million

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.380
budget, according to Box Office Mojo.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:11.250
However, according to PopMatters, it got rejuvenated
through home video platforms including VHS

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:12.269
and DVD.

00:13:12.269 --> 00:13:16.940
Day of the Dead garnered critical praise for
Romero's unflinching perception of society,

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:20.949
as well as for its horrific gore effects produced
by horror legend Tom Savini.

00:13:20.949 --> 00:13:25.280
While Romero continued his Night of the Living
Dead series with more films over the years,

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.676
Day of the Dead capped off the best trilogy
of the bunch.

00:13:28.676 --> 00:13:32.600
You're Next is a modern slasher film that
takes the Scream and Halloween murder games

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:33.770
to the next level.

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:38.269
Erin is a young Australian woman who accompanies
her boyfriend Crispin Davison to his family

00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:40.870
reunion at their vacation home in Missouri.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:44.370
The family aren't exactly friendly or agreeable
with one another.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:48.380
Before long, masked killers begin assaulting
the home, killing everyone one by one.

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:52.070
Erin attempts to survive the onslaught of
terror with all the skills she has, as she

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:56.170
gets to the bottom of the heinous plan carried
out against the Davison family.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:14:00.170
The film features over-the-top death traps
and merciless assaults with plenty of blood

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:01.490
to go around.

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:05.400
You're Next received mostly positive reviews
from the critics thanks to its fresh take

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.880
on the "final girl" trope.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:11.660
Rotten Tomatoes Critics' Consensus praised
the film in particular, calling it an "energetic

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.690
and effective mix of brutal gore and pitch
black humor."

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:18.120
"I just think that is just the height of the
artform these days."

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:24.649
However, it didn't exactly land well at the
box office, only making $26 million worldwide,

00:14:24.649 --> 00:14:26.329
according to Box Office Mojo.

00:14:26.329 --> 00:14:30.180
The film has gotten more attention since its
home video release and placement on various

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:31.650
streaming services.

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:35.500
Like several of these beloved cult horror
films, You're Next has benefited from the

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:40.130
modern convenience of in-home streaming services,
which has turned it into a modern slasher

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:41.509
that many people love.

00:14:41.509 --> 00:14:45.670
It's also a notable stepping stone in director
Adam Wingard's career, who's since gone on

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:50.000
to direct much larger productions such as
Godzilla vs. Kong.

00:14:50.449 --> 00:14:54.579
John Carpenter's The Thing tells the story
of a hostile alien infiltrating a crew in

00:14:54.579 --> 00:14:57.100
Antarctica on a microscopic level.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:01.259
The alien's cells assimilate the host they've
infected and replace the living organism's

00:15:01.259 --> 00:15:02.899
cells with its own.

00:15:02.899 --> 00:15:06.910
Led by MacReady, played by Kurt Russell, the
crew attempts to figure out who's real and

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:09.040
who's a hideous alien in disguise.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:13.730
The growing confusion creates an unsettling
fear and sense of anxiety and dread that's

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:15.959
largely unmatched in the horror genre.

00:15:15.959 --> 00:15:19.920
Despite the film's status as a horror legend
these days, it was instantly dismissed by

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.930
critics upon its release in 1982.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:26.940
Many were put off by the gore and practical
effects that accentuate the visceral grotesque

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:31.610
horror of the alien Thing, and they believed
the film to be a soulless cash-in on the attraction

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:33.420
to excess violence in cinema.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:37.509
Furthermore, the plot was accused of being
stale and too simplistic.

00:15:37.509 --> 00:15:41.110
Alan Spencer wrote for Starlog Magazine that
The Thing is a "little more than two hours

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:46.802
of makeup test footage" and heavily criticized
Carpenter for the film's lack of story and vision.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:51.410
Evidently, audiences were also turned off
by Carpenter's body horror, as Box Office

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:56.360
Mojo reports the film made just $19.6 million
at the box office.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:59.259
In the modern age, the film has been regarded
quite differently.

00:15:59.259 --> 00:16:03.699
Many have revisited its cultural impact on
horror, with Wired calling it a "paranoid

00:16:03.699 --> 00:16:06.829
classic" and Inverse claiming that it raised
the bar for sci-fi.

00:16:06.829 --> 00:16:11.589
Guillermo del Toro wrote a series of tweets
praising Carpenter and The Thing, writing,

00:16:18.285 --> 00:16:23.170
Slither is an over-the-top black comedy horror
film directed and written by James Gunn that

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.870
does not disappoint in the entertainment department.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:30.170
Whether you come away from this flick hating
it or loving it, there's never a dull moment.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:35.320
A meteorite introduces an alien parasite into
a small town in South Carolina, which promptly

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.520
infects a man named Grant Grant.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:42.259
The alien assumes control of his body and
is able to hideously transform and sprout

00:16:42.259 --> 00:16:43.259
tentacles.

00:16:43.259 --> 00:16:47.139
The possessed Grant Grant abducts a woman
and infects her, forcing her to breed alien

00:16:47.139 --> 00:16:48.139
larvae.

00:16:48.139 --> 00:16:52.579
When the police get involved, the townsfolk
focus on locating Grant and stopping the transformation

00:16:52.579 --> 00:16:54.220
of local residents.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:59.480
Although it was a critical success, Slither
was a box office failure, raking in only $12.8

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:01.829
million against a $15 million budget.

00:17:01.829 --> 00:17:06.470
In fact, the reports of the film's financial
failure were so abysmal that The Hollywood

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:10.559
Reporter speculated that the film may spell
the end for the horror-comedy sub-genre for

00:17:10.559 --> 00:17:11.559
quite some time.

00:17:11.559 --> 00:17:15.900
In the same article, horror director Eli Roth
commented on the dicey nature of selling a

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:20.000
horror-comedy film, but he noted the potential
for longevity, saying,

00:17:27.271 --> 00:17:31.230
Maybe we're watching it on streaming services
now instead of DVD, but he's not wrong.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:36.270
It only took a decade for retrospectives to
start churning out reevaluations of the film,

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:39.919
with publications like Entertainment Weekly
calling it a cult classic.

